        'text' => "The Red Death had long devastated the country. No plague was ever so fatal, for its victims bled at the pores and died within half an hour of the first signs.\n\nPrince Prospero was happy and fearless. When half his people had died he summoned a thousand friends and shut them within one of his castellated abbeys.\n\nThe gates were welded shut, and within were every pleasure and every safeguard. The world might take care of itself.\n\nAfter five or six months of seclusion he gave a masked ball of rare splendor.\n\nThe revels were held in seven rooms set in a long winding suite. Each room was stained a different color, blue, purple, green, orange, white, and violet in turn.\n\nThe seventh room was hung in black velvet, and its windows glowed scarlet, deep red like blood.\n\nIn that chamber stood a great ebony clock. When its hour struck the musicians paused and the whole company shivered, for its voice was thick and dreamlike.\n\nAt midnight came a figure no guest had seen before. Tall and gaunt, it wore garments of grave clothes and a mask like the stiff face of a corpse stricken by the Red Death.\n\nProspero trembled with rage at the insult. He hurried through the six rooms with a drawn dagger while his guests cowered in terror.\n\nIn the black chamber the masked figure turned and faced him. There was a sharp cry, and the dagger fell upon the sable carpet as the prince dropped dead.\n\nA crowd surged in and seized the intruder, but under the shroud they found nothing. Every garment and every mask was empty.\n\nAnd one by one the revelers died in the halls of their refuge, for Darkness and Decay and the Red Death held illimitable dominion over all.",
