        'text' => "The wound in my side was deep, so my servant Pedro found shelter for us in one of the gloomy chateaux of the Apennines.\n\nWe lodged in a small tower bedchamber hung with rich black velvet and crowded with armor and old paintings.\n\nI read far into the night in a small book I found there, which told the history of all the pictures upon the wall.\n\nMoving my candelabrum slowly, I came at last upon a portrait that had escaped my notice, a simple oval showing a young girl just ripening into womanhood.\n\nI closed my eyes that I might think calmly over what I had seen, and then a sudden wonder seized me. The painting seemed absolutely alive.\n\nI had seen many faces in that room, yet this one held me. The artist had caught not merely shape and color but the very warmth of breath behind them.\n\nEagerly I searched for the passage about it in my book. There I read the strange story the frame concealed.\n\nShe was the bride of the painter, a maiden rarer than art. He loved beauty and she loved him, and she smiled at the thought that his brush could honor even her.\n\nDay after day she sat meekly in the tall dark chamber while the colors grew upon the canvas. Her spirits wasted as her likeness flourished, but he would not see it.\n\nHe painted hands, eyes, and last the faint breath of the lips, and grew wild with joy at his own work.\n\nAt length he cried aloud that this was Life itself. He turned toward his beloved and saw that she was dead.",
