        'synopsis' => 'A shopgirl starves eight months for a purple dress, loses her savings to a friend, and still wins her Thanksgiving miracle.',
        'text' => "Maida served behind the counter of the Bee-Hive Store, a girl with big brown eyes and cinnamon colored hair.\n\nPurple was the fashion that autumn, and Maida announced to her friend Grace that she meant to wear a tailor-made purple dress on Thanksgiving Day.\n\nGrace teased her without mercy. \"Sly boots,\" she cried. \"You think Mr. Ramsay likes purple.\"\n\nMr. Ramsay was the head clerk of the store, a kind gentleman whom all ten shopgirls dreamed of marrying, since old Bachman meant to make him partner next year.\n\n\"I prefer purple,\" said Maida, \"and those that don't like it can take the other side of the street.\"\n\nThe dress was her dream and her sacrifice together. For eight months she had pinched and saved until eighteen dollars lay in her dresser drawer, enough for the cloth and part of Schlegel's tailoring bill.\n\nFour dollars were still owed to the old tailor, and Maida meant to pay them the night before the feast and carry her dress home in triumph.\n\nThat very night she came home to clamor and confusion. Grace stood crying in the hall, put out by the landlady because she owed her four dollars of rent.\n\n\"I paid it on my red dress,\" sobbed Grace. \"I haven't a cent left in the world.\"\n\nOut came Maida's four dollars, though it broke her heart to part with them.\n\nOn Thanksgiving morning Grace switched into her new red gown and looked charming in it, while Maida sat by the window in her old skirt and blue waist.\n\n\"My dress didn't get finished in time,\" said Maida quietly. \"I'm not going to the dinner at all.\"\n\nAll the long afternoon she sat alone and heard in memory the merriment of the store dinner where Mr. Ramsay moved about kindly among his people.\n\nAt four o'clock she walked to Schlegel's shop and owned sadly that she could not pay the money due him.\n\nThe old tailor looked at her worn face and pressed the finished dress into her arms. \"So,\" he said gruffly. \"Pay me when you can. It is made good, and if you look pretty in it, all right.\"\n\nRain met Maida at the door, and she did not feel it. She dressed in her purple and walked abroad through the storm with a serene and happy face.\n\nGirls whose wardrobes come charged to a rich father's account cannot understand what it means to starve eight months to bring a purple dress and a holiday together.\n\nSomeone stepped from a corner and blocked her way. It was Mr. Ramsay, and his eyes sparkled with admiration.\n\n\"Miss Maida,\" he said warmly, \"you look simply magnificent. Nothing is more healthful than braving the weather as you do. May I walk with you?\"\n\nAnd Maida blushed and sneezed.",
