        'cover' => 'covers/scifi-davidsons-eyes.jpg',
        'synopsis' => 'After a laboratory accident Sidney Davidson goes blind to London yet watches a distant sea island where real events unfold.',
        'text' => "My friend Sidney Davidson was a quiet student of science, and no man was less likely to become famous for a marvel.\n\nThe thing befell at Harlow Technical College on a wild evening of thunder and hail.\n\nDavidson stood alone in the great laboratory between the poles of the biggest electro-magnet in England when lightning struck the building.\n\nHearing glass smash, I ran in from the next room and found him swaying with dazzled eyes, groping at empty air.\n\n\"Waves,\" he muttered. \"And a remarkably neat schooner.\"\n\nHe could not see me though I stood before him. He saw instead a sunlit beach in some southern sea, with penguins staggering over the rocks and gulls wheeling overhead.\n\nHe walked straight into the magnet and bruised himself cruelly against it. To him the iron had been empty space.\n\nWe carried him to his room and sent for doctors, but medicine could do nothing. His eyes were sound, and his mind was clear on every point except the one that mattered most.\n\nFor three weeks he remained stone blind to everything around him in London while that other scene stayed fixed before his sight.\n\nFriends led him about the city and he described what passed before him, the rocks, the whales that showed offshore, the boat whose men landed and waited out the storm.\n\nOur friend Professor Wade offered the boldest explanation. Perhaps, said he, the lightning had given a kink to space itself, bending two distant points together like a folded sheet of paper.\n\nThrough that twist Davidson's sight might travel to the very antipodes of the earth while his feet stayed planted in our own hemisphere.\n\nRecovery began strangely. One morning a patch of the real world appeared within the vision like a hole in the phantom sky, and the patches spread day by day until the island faded altogether.\n\nAt first the two pictures confused him terribly, overlapping like views in a magic lantern, but in a little while he learned again to feed himself and read and walk the streets like an ordinary citizen.\n\nSoon afterward he married my sister, and the last glimpse of his southern shore came on a night shortly before the wedding.\n\nTwo years later a naval lieutenant named Atkins dined with us and showed us a photograph of his ship, the Fulmar.\n\nDavidson started. \"I have seen that ship,\" he said. \"She stood off an island swarming with penguins, and she fired a gun.\"\n\nAtkins turned pale, for on the very day of the accident his boat crew had landed on a rock south of Antipodes Island and been kept overnight by a thunderstorm.\n\nEvery detail matched what Davidson had reported weeks before any ship brought news home.\n\nHow his sight crossed eight thousand miles remains a mystery. Wade still talks of the fourth dimension, and I confess I cannot follow him, but the facts themselves stand beyond doubt.",
