        'synopsis' => 'Andy Donovan courts a grieving girl whose dead count hides a secret that turns her confession into a happy ending.',
        'text' => "Andy Donovan boarded at Mrs. Scott's house on Second Avenue, a cheerful young fellow on the way up in politics.\n\nWhen Miss Conway arrived he marked her down as plain and dull, for she wore a snuffy brown dress and gave all her attention to her plate.\n\nMrs. Scott introduced them at dinner, and he bowed with the graceful smile that was winning him advancement, and thought no more about her.\n\nTwo weeks later she stepped onto the porch dressed wholly in black, veil, gloves, and gown, her golden hair smooth under the brim of her hat.\n\nHer gray eyes gazed into the distance with a sadness so becoming that Andy threw away a perfectly good cigar and begged to know her sorrow.\n\n\"Death has claimed one who was very dear to me,\" she murmured, and consented to a stroll in the park.\n\nThrough the gates of the old downtown park they wandered to a quiet bench, for grief shared with youth is already half lightened.\n\nOn that bench she opened her heart. Her fiancé, she said, had been a real Italian nobleman, Count Fernando Mazzini, owner of a castle in his own land.\n\nHer father had opposed the match at first, and once the lovers ran away together, only to be overtaken and brought home.\n\nAt last all was forgiven, and the wedding was set for spring. The count sailed for Italy to prepare his castle, and three days ago there came a letter telling how a gondola accident had ended his life.\n\nShe drew a locket from her dress and showed Andy the picture of a strong, cheerful, handsome man.\n\n\"My heart will remain forever in his grave,\" she said softly.\n\nNow girls, if you wish to see a young man reach for pick and shovel, tell him that your heart lies in another fellow's tomb. Young men are grave robbers by nature.\n\nAndy played the sympathetic friend so well that ice cream was shared before the evening ended, and within a month they announced their engagement to Mrs. Scott and the boarders, though Maggie still wore her mourning black.\n\nOne moonlit evening on the same bench Andy sat gloomy and silent, and love wrung the truth out of him at last.\n\nHe told her of Big Mike Sullivan, the biggest man in New York, the giant of Tammany Hall who could fill a street corner with a million men, his friend and hero, who had shaken his hand on the Bowery that very day and promised to come to the wedding.\n\n\"There is a reason why he must not be there,\" Andy said heavily, \"but don't ask me what it is.\"\n\nThen his trouble overflowed. \"Maggie, do you think as much of me as you did of Count Mazzini?\"\n\nShe waited long, and then leaned on his shoulder and shook with sobs.\n\n\"Andy, I've lied to you. There never was so much as the little finger of a count. I never had a beau in my life, but the other girls had them, and talking about beaus seemed to make the fellows like them more.\"\n\n\"And I look swell in black, you know I do. So I bought that picture at a photograph store, had a little one made for my locket, and invented the whole sad tale so that I might wear mourning. Nobody can love a liar.\"\n\nInstead of pushing her away Andy folded her closer, and his face cleared like sunshine after rain.\n\n\"Sure it's all right,\" he said. \"Back to the cemetery for the count. You've straightened everything out, bully girl.\"\n\n\"But Andy,\" she asked shyly, \"did you believe my story about him?\"\n\n\"Well, not to any large extent,\" said Andy, reaching for his cigar case, \"because it's Big Mike Sullivan's picture you've got in that locket of yours.\"",
