        'cover' => 'covers/nature-raggylug.jpg',
        'synopsis' => 'Molly Cottontail raises Rag through swamp dangers and gives her life so her son may live to rule Olifant\'s Swamp.',
        'text' => "In Olifant's Swamp, hidden in a nest of rank grass, a young cottontail was born, and the world took notice of him hardly at all.\n\nHe won his name in his first adventure. Raising his head too soon from the nest he came face to face with an enormous black serpent, which seized him by the ear and began to coil around him.\n\n\"Mammy,\" he screamed, and straight as an arrow his mother came bounding through the woods.\n\nNo shy shadow of a rabbit now, Molly Cottontail leaped over the monster and struck down at it with her sharp hind claws, again and yet again, until torn and stung the snake let go.\n\nThe baby wriggled free with a ragged torn ear for a keepsake, and followed the white beacon of his mother's tail to safety. From that day he was Raggylug, or Rag.\n\nMolly brought him up carefully, and his schooling was thorough.\n\nHe learned to lie low whatever happens, for stillness is the rabbit's shield. He learned that the briers are friends, that ground holes are traps for the unwary, and that the thump of a hind foot is a signal drum to warn every cousin in the swamp.\n\nHe learned the trick of playing frozen while enemies pass, and the wisdom of never running until the proper moment, and then running with all his might.\n\nBest of all she taught him the water road, for a rabbit can swim, and a swimming rabbit leaves no scent upon the ground.\n\nUnder such teaching Rag throve, and in his second year he was master of the dear old Swamp.\n\nMore than once his lessons saved him. Hunted by dogs, he led his pursuers a long tearing course through the briers until their tender ears were torn and bleeding, and more than one hound met ruin on the barbed wire of the old pen while Rag slipped home unhurt.\n\nBut the world of men was closing in around the little kingdom. Old Olifant cut down the wood about the pond and burned the brush piles east and south, and the cottontails' domain shrank on every side while winter came down bitter and bare.\n\nOne icy night a fox stole up wind to the brush heap where mother and son lay snug.\n\nMolly darted out first and led the chase away, and Rag, escaping, doubled back to change off and draw the hunter after himself.\n\nShe gained the pond margin and plunged in. The fox plunged too, but icy water was more than Reynard cared for, and he turned back growling.\n\nMolly swam on against a headwind, through reeds and floating snow that blocked her like soft ice.\n\nHer brave strokes slowed, her limbs grew numb, and when at last she reached the far reeds the ice forming around her stopped her altogether.\n\nIn a little while the little mother Cottontail was asleep in the arms of the water that tells no tales.\n\nRag sought her everywhere, trailed and thumped and called, but he never found her and never knew where she went.\n\nPoor little Molly was a true heroine, one of unnumbered millions who live and do their best and die without a thought of heroism.\n\nShe lives on in Rag, who carried her courage and her cunning into every fight of his days.\n\nOld Olifant died that winter, and the swamp ran wild again. Brambles grew, fallen fences made fresh castles, and there to this day lives Rag, a big strong buck with a family of his own and a pretty brown wife that he got nobody knows where.\n\nOn any sunny evening, if you have learned their signal code and know just how and when to thump it, you may see his children flourishing in the old home of their race.",
