        'slug' => 'nature-redruff',
        'gutenberg_id' => 3031,
        'title' => 'Redruff the Partridge',
        'author' => 'Ernest Thompson Seton',
        'license' => 'Public domain',
        'genres' => ['Science/Nature'],
        'quiz_difficulty' => 'hard',
        'cover' => 'covers/nature-redruff.jpg',
        'synopsis' => 'Redruff the partridge outwits foxes, hawks, and hunters across the Don Valley until a snare ends his famous drumming.',
        'text' => "Down the wooded slope of Taylor's Hill the mother partridge led her twelve newly hatched brood toward the crystal brook that by some strange whim was called Mud Creek.\n\nThe woods were full of enemies, and she watched every bush while the tiny balls of mottled down came toddling after her clucking call.\n\nAcross the beaver meadow a fox was coming their way. One low firm note from the mother meant hide, and instantly every chick vanished under leaves and bark curls, all but one fat fellow who squatted flat on a yellow chip and shut his eyes very tight.\n\nThen the mother flew straight toward the fox and flung herself down before him, flopping as though dreadfully lame and whining like a hurt puppy.\n\nReynard sprang and missed, sprang again, and always the helpless bird dragged herself just beyond his jaws, leading him farther from her hidden little ones until at last she rose with a derisive whirr and flew off, leaving the fool to remember how often he had been served that same trick.\n\nThe biggest chick on the chip was the hero of this history, and his mother's cleverness kept all twelve alive through their tenderest days.\n\nThey learned to freeze at the warning note, to scatter and trust stillness, and to run to her spread tail for shade from the sun.\n\nOne noonday a hawk's shadow swept the slope, and at the mother's cry every chick froze where it stood, so perfectly still that the killer's eye passed them by as stones.\n\nSeasons turned and peril taught its hard lessons. When the shooting moons came hunters tramped the hills, hawks stooped from the sky, and of the twelve brothers and sisters few remained by the first frost.\n\nBut the chick on the chip grew into the grandest partridge of the Don Valley, splendid in copper and rainbow ruffs, famous up and down the woods for the thunder of his drumming on the old pine log.\n\nWhen the pairing moon came he drummed morning and evening, a deep throbbing salute that rolled down the valley, and at last a gentle mate called Graytail stood beside him with her head coyly lifted.\n\nTheir worst enemy proved to be a sullen poacher named Cuddy, a shambling ill-favored man who hunted the ravine winter and summer with dog and gun and grudged every bird his lead missed.\n\nMany a snapshot he tried, but Redruff knew woodcraft better than any bird alive, knowing when to squat till nearly trodden on, then rise behind a tree trunk with a roar of wings and skim away in safety.\n\nThen came the storm of storms. Snow fell soft and deep, then froze to crust, and the birds, buried alive in their drift, could only peck upward in the dark.\n\nFor two days they pecked at the icy ceiling above their heads, growing weaker, while hunger gnawed and the drift held them like a tomb.\n\nRedruff bored patiently until his head broke into the open air, and freeing himself at last he flew to fetch red hips for food and returned to dig out his own.\n\nGraytail answered his clucking feebly and crawled forth alive. Of all the rest scattered through the drifts no sign or sound ever came again.\n\nSpring found the pair hunted harder than ever, but a great pine six feet through stood on the east slope among the hemlocks, and a dozen times its mighty trunk rose between the gun and the birds, letting them rise behind it untouched.\n\nCuddy laid ambush at last beneath the bank while a companion drove the thicket. Graytail caught sight of the yellow cur and rose in panic, flying from safety straight into the power waiting under the bank.\n\nUp she went, a beautiful and noble being. A gun spoke, and down she fell, to gasp her life out on the snow.\n\nRedruff heard the shot from the feeding ground and called and called, but no answering whistle came, and he lived alone thereafter through the Stormy Moon.\n\nWhen deep snow and scarce food made guns useless Cuddy set a row of snares across the feeding ground.\n\nA friendly cottontail cut several of these with sharp teeth, but some remained, and Redruff, watching a far speck that might be a hawk, trod in one and was jerked into the air to hang by one foot.\n\nAll that day and all the night and through another day he hung, beating his strong wings in helpless agony and longing only for death.\n\nNo human eye saw him, for the place was far from any path. In the darkest hours a great horned owl came silently down the ravine, drawn by the feeble flutter below, and cut short the pain, and the deed was wholly kind.\n\nThe north wind swept the valley and bore away on it plumy fragments of the famous rainbow ruffs, the last trace of the last of the Don Valley race.\n\nNo partridge comes to Castle Frank now. The wood birds miss the martial spring salute, and in Mud Creek Ravine the old pine drumlog, since unused, has rotted in silence away.",
